{
  "requirement": "When implementation shows end-user-observable behavior no requirement covers, the `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to draft a requirement for it and take it through review, agreement, and commit before implementing that behavior.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligation.** The `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents, when implementation shows end-user-observable behavior no requirement covers, to (a) draft a requirement for it, (b) take it through review, (c) take it through agreement, and (d) take it through commit, all before implementing that behavior.\n\n**Skill text.** I read `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md`. The Workflow closing paragraph says: \"Behavior no requirement covers goes back to step 2.\" The steps then run as follows:\n- Step 2 is \"Draft.\"\n- Step 3 is \"Review.\"\n- Step 4 is \"Present each group to the user\", followed by a **Gate** that allows no implementation or test code and no commit until the user has agreed, rejected or deferred every group.\n- Step 5 is \"Commit ... all groups together, before any code.\"\n- Step 6 is \"Implement and test.\"\n\n**Test mapping.**\n- `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_drafts_a_requirement_for_uncovered_behavior]` asserts the redirect phrase \"Behavior no requirement covers goes back to step 2\" through `assert_phrases`. It asserts that step 2 starts with \"Draft.\", step 3 starts with \"Review.\", and step 4 starts with \"Present each group to the user\". Together these cover the draft, review and agreement parts (a, b, c). The step-number lookup helpers `step` and `step_of` panic if the step is missing, so the assertions are not vacuous.\n- `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_commits_every_group_together_before_code]` finds the step containing \"then commit it, all groups together, before any code\". It asserts that this step's text contains \"leave out unrelated edits found in step 1\", and that its number is greater than the step containing \"**Gate:**\". This covers the commit part (d) and the ordering of commit after agreement and before code.\n\n**Gaps considered.** Neither test asserts that the implement step comes after the commit step, and neither ties the redirect sentence to its \"If implementation shows...\" context. The \"before any code\" phrase in step 5 and the Gate wording in step 4 already enforce the before-implementation ordering. The phrase assertion pins the sentence that carries the redirect. A skill rewrite that dropped the redirect, or that sent uncovered behavior anywhere other than the draft, review, agreement and commit sequence, would fail one of these assertions. I found no requirement-violating wording that both tests would accept.\n\nNo acknowledgments apply.",
  "references": "40118cebcd0ae82f9a3c66be832c1eb5124d409e710d7e092e5d442046a8eb42",
